(project pricklypear-single-image
 (doc "Book chapter: the single-image process model. One unix
       binary, one Postgres, one deployment. Multi-user at the
       session/branch layer, not process isolation. This chapter
       is the authority for process topology; an earlier
       multi-process design (supervisor + authd + per-user
       unikernels) was retired.")
 (status implemented)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section single-image
  (doc "One unix image process: model, topology, auth placement, deployment")
  (status implemented)

  (subsection thesis
   (doc "What the system IS")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Pricklypear is ONE OCaml unix binary — the image — that:

        1. Serves htmx HTTP directly on a port (Httpun_eio
           + pure-html). This is the only user-facing surface.
        2. Talks to Postgres via pure pgx over Eio.Net (Io_eio).
           Postgres is the durable object store; the image is
           ephemeral and rebuildable from the store plus the
           package seed (libs/*.pp -> system/main).
        3. Reads its identity (branch, author) from process env
           at boot — no boot manifest, no external injection.
        4. Optionally exposes a line-protocol REPL on a second
           port for operator access. The browser REPL (/repl)
           and HTTP eval (/api/eval) are the primary
           interactive surfaces.

      Scope — what the system is NOT:

        - Not a MirageOS unikernel. No Solo5 target in v1.
          `dune build` + restart is how the image changes.
          (Solo5 remains an optional later hardening spike —
          spikes/mirage_solo5/ — not the product path.)
        - Not multi-process. No supervisor, no authd sidecar,
          no fleet. The image is the only Pricklypear process.
        - Isolation between humans lives at the session and
          branch layer, not the process layer. There is no
          per-user image instance.

      Rationale: one process cannot fail the ways a
      coordinated multi-process topology can — no port races
      on respawn, no stale route tables, no orphaned children,
      no cross-process identity handoff.
    |)))
  )

  (subsection topology
   (doc "The physical layout")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      OpenBSD host (build + runtime habitat):

        +-- Postgres (shared object store, SERIALIZABLE
        |              defaults)
        |
        +-- pricklypear image (ONE unix binary)
        |   +-- Httpun_eio on PP_HTTP_PORT (htmx UI,
        |   |   /api/eval, /repl, user routes)
        |   +-- optional line-protocol REPL on
        |   |   PP_REPL_PORT (operator only; Lwt_eio bridge)
        |   +-- Io_eio.Pg to Postgres (function_defs,
        |       data_rows, branches, auth tables)
        |
        +-- nginx (edge)
            +-- TLS -> proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:PP_HTTP_PORT

      Flow for "user adds a function":
        1. Open the browser (nginx front door).
        2. /repl or the function editor: the define persists
           to function_defs and is registered live.
        3. Routes serve it immediately.
    |)))
  )

  (subsection auth
   (doc "Where verify runs")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Verify runs IN the image. Password and session verify
      happen in-process against the users, credentials, and
      auth_sessions tables; the /login cookie middleware
      guards the HTTP surface. No separate authd exists.

      nginx basic-auth/htpasswd is an emergency edge gate
      only — it is NOT the user database.

      Multi-user (many humans, sessions, personal garden
      branches) is a product layer above this process model;
      see auth.multi-user-product for the user stories.
    |)))
  )

  (subsection runtime
   (doc "Runtime effects: direct-style core, algebraic-effect rim, Eio floor")
   (status implemented)
   (implements
     lisp/lib/control.ml
     image/lib/control_rim.ml
     image/lib/primitives.ml
     image/lib/store_db.ml
     image/lib/io.mli
     image/lib/io_lwt.ml
     image/lib/io_eio.ml
     image/lib/crud.ml
     image/lib/tree_store.ml
     image/lib/session.ml
     image/lib/http.ml
     image/lib/boot.ml)
   (verify
     (build "dune build && dune runtest")
     (test "image/test/io_eio_test.ml")
     (smoke "image serves /health and a CRUD roundtrip under PP_IO=eio")
     (smoke "(with-transaction (lambda () (create ...))) commits; select-for-update inside body sees the lock, on the default backend"))
   (details
    (doc (|
      Three layers — never collapse them (the two \"effects\"):

        A. GUEST CAPABILITIES — what a Nopales expression may
           touch (pure / read-db / write-db / branch / publish
           / ai / html / txn). Static AST inference in
           lisp/lib/effect.ml; drives badges and gates.
           Spec: lisp-interpreter.effects. NOT this section.
        B. HOST CONTROL EFFECTS — how eval does I/O without
           monads. OCaml 5 Effect.perform of one effect:
             Prim_call : string * value list -> value Effect.t
           declared in lisp/lib/control.ml. Guest Nopales can
           never perform it; eval's prim dispatch is the only
           performer.
        C. I/O RUNTIME — who schedules sockets/DB/fibers.
           Lwt today; Eio is the floor we are moving to.
           The power company; chosen last, replaceable first.

      This subsection is the book authority for layers B and C.
      The phase plan and runbook live in
      docs/runtime-effects-transition.md (H0 audit -> H1
      direct-style core -> H2 Io port -> H3 backend swap);
      that doc is the runbook, this is the law.

      LAYER B — HOST CONTROL EFFECTS (H1, SHIPPED):

        - lisp/ is direct-style: Eval.eval returns value, no
          monadic types, no Lwt dependency.
        - Eval's prim dispatch performs Prim_call(name, args);
          prim bodies do NOT recurse into eval (guest lambdas
          needing DB access go through with-transaction's
          dynamic scope instead).
        - Pure tests install Control.run_sync with a sync prim
          table: service-free, no rim.
        - The image runs every eval under Control_rim.run
          (Effect.Deep.match_with): Prim_call looks up the
          registered prim fn, runs it, continues (or
          discontinues on raise). Pure lisp/ tests stay green
          with no services.

      LAYER C — IO PORT + EIO FLOOR (SHIPPED):

        - Process entry is always Eio_main + Httpun_eio accept
          loop. Domain SQL goes through Io.DB (io.mli) →
          store_db.ml → Io_eio.Pg (pure pgx over Eio.Net).
          scripts/check_io_rim.sh enforces the rim.
        - Store/crud/auth/session/agent_tools are direct-style:
          plain 'a returns, prim_fn = value list -> value,
          register_sync everywhere. DB calls block the calling
          fiber; a reentrant per-connection mutex serializes
          whole transactions across concurrent fibers.
        - HTTP page work is Fiber.fork'd off the connection
          read fiber so body reads (schedule_read) cannot
          deadlock against Promise.await.
        - Prim registration peeled into prims_request /
          prims_net / prims_media / prims_graph / prims_db;
          session.ml keeps t, rehydrate, checkout, eval,
          seed_stdlib, and register_session_control_prims.
        - Residual Lwt is rim-only: optional TCP REPL and
          curl-backed llm/http-get/sleep via Lwt_eio bridges.
          A later pass can replace those without touching
          domain code.

      DYNAMIC TRANSACTION SCOPE (preserved):

        - Store_db.with_dynamic_transaction binds current_tx
          around a thunk; crud execute paths prefer current_tx
          over the pool conn, so all prims in a
          (with-transaction (lambda () ...)) body share one
          SERIALIZABLE tx and select-for-update locks persist
          across prims in the body. Nested with-transaction
          fails (no silent flattening). Binding is per-fiber
          under the conn mutex, never ambient-global.

      RIM LINT:

        - scripts/check_io_rim.sh stays green: only store_db /
          io_eio (and legacy io_lwt name) call pgx execute.

      HARD INVARIANTS (every horizon, restated):

        1. Composition: (define u (get ...)) then (str u)
           yields a plain value, never a promise.
        2. (with-transaction ...) dynamic scope as above.
        3. Budget/cancel: one bad eval cannot wedge the image
           (budget ticks at each eval step AND at each rim
           prim dispatch).
        4. Pure lisp/ tests stay service-free; image smokes
           stay green on the default backend.
        5. Capability inference (layer A) unchanged; guest
           Nopales never gains Effect.perform.
    |)))
  )

  (subsection deployment
   (doc "One binary, one rc.d service, one restart")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
      rc.d service `pricklypear' (scripts/rc.d/pricklypear):
        start   -> /usr/local/libexec/pricklypear/current/bin/image
                   with env from /etc/pricklypear/pricklypear.env
        stop    -> kill the single PID
        restart -> stop + start (one process, one port,
                   clean shutdown)

      Release promotion (git push to prod):
        1. build the release (lisp + common + image)
        2. extract to /usr/local/libexec/pricklypear/releases/<id>
        3. swap the `current' symlink
        4. rcctl restart pricklypear

      (Full hook/runbook detail: docs/openbsd-prod-branch-deploy.md.)

      Changing a primitive: edit image/lib, push, the promote
      step rebuilds and restarts. Changing an app: define or
      route on a branch in the live image — no deploy at all.
    |)))
  )
 )
)
